;; Q: Which drugs reach Parkinson disease by a sign-consistent mechanism -- the drug
;;    regulates a gene, the gene drives a phenotype, the phenotype drives the disease --
;;    such that the composed effect LOWERS the disease?
;;
;; SIGN ALGEBRA.  signed_edge carries sigma in {+1,-1}: "an increase in the subject
;; produces an increase (+1) or a decrease (-1) in the object".  A path is therapeutic
;; exactly when the product of its hop signs is -1.  That is two clauses, not an
;; enumeration of sign combinations:
;;     (= prod (* s1 (* s2 s3)))  (= prod -1)
;; Picking +1 on hop 1 therefore FORCES an odd number of -1 downstream, which is the
;; direction lock.  Both hop-3 modes are admitted: mode="causation" is evidence, while
;; mode="membership" is the convention that a phenotype OF a disease tracks it (+1) --
;; so a membership row is a SYMPTOMATIC claim, not a disease-modifying one.  m3 is
;; returned so that distinction survives into the answer.
;;
;; UNSIGNED EDGES ARE ABSENT BY CONSTRUCTION.  gene_associated_with_condition (1.03M
;; edges), related_to, correlated_with and biomarker_for carry no direction and are not
;; in signed_edge.  Nothing here silently assumes they are positive.
;;
;; CATEGORIES.  node_kind, not node.  KG2 categories are flat and single-valued, and
;; most drug-target edges land on biolink:Polypeptide / biolink:Protein rather than
;; biolink:Gene, while most chemical subjects are biolink:ChemicalEntity rather than
;; biolink:Drug.  Filtering on "biolink:Gene" alone would drop ~90% of hop 1.
;;
;; BRIDGES.  kg2_norm between every independently-derived hop.  The gene reached from the
;; drug is a UniProtKB/PR node; the gene reaching the phenotype is an NCBIGene/HGNC node.
;; Joining those on the raw id returns 0 rows (measured: naive 0, bridged 125).  The
;; disease anchor is bridged too, which covers all its KG2 ids in one clause instead of
;; one literal rule per id.
;;
;; STAGES.  Two rules, one head, so ONE derived relation.  signed_edge, node_kind, node
;; and kg2_norm are all base views built at session open and add nothing.  Predicted
;; 9s compile + 13s = ~22s; docs/NOTES.md's real per-stratum figure puts it nearer 60-90s.
;;
;; ROW SHAPE.  The head is projected to (depth, drug, name) rather than to full paths:
;; Slog deduplicates, so a narrower head IS the aggregation and costs nothing, and it
;; turns tens of thousands of path rows into one row per drug.  The 2-hop rows are tagged
;; rather than ranked -- which 50 rows come back is the daemon's storage order, not ours.

table (kg2_norm str str)
table (node str str str)
table (node_kind str str)
table (signed_edge str int str str str)

;; depth  drug  drug_name  hop3_mode
table (result str str str str)

;; ---- 3 hops: drug -> gene -> phenotype -> Parkinson disease -----------------
rule (result "3hop" drug dname m3) <--
  (node_kind drug "chemical")
  (signed_edge drug s1 x1 _ "regulation")
  (node_kind x1 "gene")
  (kg2_norm x1 gkey)
  (kg2_norm x2 gkey)
  (node_kind x2 "gene")
  (signed_edge x2 s2 p1 _ "causation")
  (node_kind p1 "phenotype")
  (kg2_norm p1 pkey)
  (kg2_norm p2 pkey)
  (signed_edge p2 s3 d1 _ m3)
  (kg2_norm d1 "MONDO:0005180")
  (node drug dname _)
  (= prod (* s1 (* s2 s3)))
  (= prod -1)

;; ---- 2 hops: drug -> gene -> Parkinson disease, same head, depth-tagged -----
rule (result "2hop" drug dname "direct") <--
  (node_kind drug "chemical")
  (signed_edge drug s1 x1 _ "regulation")
  (node_kind x1 "gene")
  (kg2_norm x1 gkey)
  (kg2_norm x2 gkey)
  (node_kind x2 "gene")
  (signed_edge x2 s2 d1 _ "causation")
  (kg2_norm d1 "MONDO:0005180")
  (node drug dname _)
  (= prod (* s1 s2))
  (= prod -1)
